## Why converged
1. **Host/launch axis is exhausted.** The geomean is dominated by the 17 launch-bound
   tiny/micro workloads (`total_q<=92`), which are pinned at their ~0.05ms single-launch
   floor and unchanged across c003/c004/c005. The three big host-side wins (kill the
   per-seq Python loop + `.item()` syncs via one fused kernel; remove the residual
   `max().item()` sync on small inputs; drop the two init memsets) are all banked in c004.
2. **GPU-efficiency axis is saturated.** The 3 large cases (#5/#6/#20) and #17 are the
   only compute-bound workloads. `num_stages=3` (c004) is the pipeline optimum; both
   remaining branch-A knobs regressed: `BLOCK_N=128` (c005, −0.06 — the loop was already
   latency-hidden, larger tiles add register/smem pressure) and `num_warps=4` (c006,
   −0.14 — 4 warps under-feed the tensor cores: #5 34.4→28.5, #6 36.4→28.3, #20 31.6→25.3).
3. **Returns have plateaued:** c002→c003 +1.44, c003→c004 +0.22, c004→c005 −0.06,
   c004→c006 −0.14. No attributable next-candidate hypothesis remains with positive
   expected geomean value:
   - Precision (branch H3) unnecessary: all 21 pass comfortably with bf16-dot + fp32 accum.
   - Ragged schedule V2 (branch C) targets load imbalance on the large multi-seq cases,
     but those are already 25–37x and are only 3 of 21 (tiny-dominated geomean ⇒ minimal
     upside), and V1's dense grid already early-exits over-length blocks cheaply.
   - GQA K/V-reuse V3 (branch D) offers no real traffic win under causal masking (finer
     position-blocking cancels the 4× head-reuse; the 4 same-kv-head CTAs already share
     K/V via L2) and risks regression — rejected on analysis, not worth an eval.

